================
A caricature portraying two Mexican men on a fraternity T-shirt was found
to be constitutionally protected speech at the University of California at
Riverside.
[...]
Maura Whalen, spokesperson for the Individual Rights Foundation, a group 
concerned with First Amendment issues on college campuses, said her 
organization offered to legally represent the fraternity after the chapter
was ordered to disband for three years.
[...]

But an out of court settlement was reached when the Individual Rights
Foundation filed suit against the university and the issue was changed
from one of "behavioral problems of young men" [, the univerity's
perspective, -cmk] to a violation of First Amendment rights, Chappell
said.
[....]
The settlement called for the reinstatement of the fraternity and First 
Amendment sensitivity training for Assistant Vice Chancellor Vincent Del 
Pizzo and activities director Kevin Ferguson, Whalen said.
[...]
But the fraternity, as part of the settlement, is required to comply with
the sanctions imposed in it by its national headquarters, including 
writing letters of apology to offended ethnic groups on campus, the other
greek organizations on campus and the national headquarters, Chappell 
said. 
